🔹 DEH – Default Exception Handling
- Provided by the JVM
- Handles exceptions automatically
- If exception occurs:
- JVM prints exception name
- description
- stack trace
- Program terminates abnormally
- Not user-friendly
Example:
int a = 10 / 0;   // JVM handles it

🔹 UDEH – User Defined Exception Handling
- Handled by the programmer
- Uses try, catch, finally, throw, throws
- Prevents abnormal termination
- Allows custom messages and recovery
Example:
 try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
}
🔹 Key Difference
DEH	                                 UDEH
JVM handles exception	      Programmer handles exception
Program crashes           	Program continues
No custom message	          Custom handling possible
